First, let me express the United States’ sincere gratitude to The Gambia for its leadership and vision in guiding the Second Committee through a long-overdue reckoning. The Chair’s willingness to tackle these institutional issues head-on is commendable, and the Bureau has helped steer us to this point.
The United States joins consensus today on this decision because we believe in efficiency, accountability, and good stewardship of resources. Trimming the UN’s bloated calendar, cutting down on redundant reports, and curbing the endless churn of annual resolutions are necessary, common-sense steps.
(U) This decision is a positive start but let me be completely clear: this is a starting point, not the finish line, and much more must be done.
By advancing these efforts, the Second Committee is setting the standard. We strongly urge other committees—from First to Sixth—to look to the Second Committee as a leader in revitalization and follow our lead in eliminating institutional waste.
While we support these procedural improvements, the United States must formally disassociate from paragraph (e) of this decision. We do not accept the narrative that the work of the Second Committee exists primarily to serve as an administrative delivery mechanism for the 2030 Agenda or top-down global development targets. This is in no way an endorsement of language that treats central planning frameworks as the benchmark for global progress. True economic development is driven by sovereign responsibility, free-market innovation, trade, and the rule of law, not by UN agendas.
For too long, the UN has drifted from its core mission, bogged down by a culture that prioritizes process over performance and paperwork over real-world economic results. The Second Committee has become a factory for “zombie resolutions” that reflect outdated economic paradigms and risk infringing upon national sovereignty. Business as usual is a luxury this institution can no longer afford.
Moving forward, the Second Committee must continue this work, and enforce efficiency across four non-negotiable pillars:
Radical Consolidation: We must eliminate thematic silos and eradicate duplicative mandates that belong elsewhere. Technical issues belong in specialized agencies in Rome, Geneva, or Nairobi, not in line-by-line technical rehashes in New York. We must merge more fragmented, niche resolutions into lean, high-impact omnibus frameworks and implement strict sunset protocols.
Periodicity and Ending the Annual Machine: Shifting stable thematic items to biennial, triennial, or quadrennial reviews is mandatory. We are here to oversee measurable results, not to subsidize a perpetual drafting machine that values the quantity of paperwork over actual impact.
Text Streamlining and Structural Limits: We must mandate hard page and word limits, eliminate pages of repetitive preambular “recalling” paragraphs, and push historical references older than five years into a single technical footnote.
Mandate Discipline and IFI Redlines: The UN must stop playing shadow regulator and respect the independent governance of international financial institutions like the IMF and World Bank. The United States firmly opposes any UN-led mechanism for debt restructuring or global taxation, and we will reject any attempt by the UN to interfere in sovereign debt negotiations or domestic laws.
The United States accepts this resolution as a step in the right direction. But a modern, effective UN requires relentless reform, absolute transparency, and unwavering respect for national sovereignty. We expect this Committee to remain at the forefront of UN reform, and we call on every other committee across this General Assembly to look to our work today as the benchmark for real revitalization.
Thank you.
