Ambassador Tammy Bruce
Deputy Representative of the United States to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you very much. My thanks to Syria, Colombia, Denmark, and Luxembourg for convening this discussion on an issue of critical importance to peace and security in Syria, and with wider implications throughout the Middle East region and beyond.
After all, peace and security relies on a government being trusted by the people. The commitment and work in finding the missing and the scrutiny that it brings is at the heart of this trust and legitimacy.
The United States thanks today’s briefers and is especially pleased that the representatives of the Syrian national commissions for transitional justice and the missing could join us today.
Their work, in partnership with the IIMP, is critical to advance the search for the missing in Syria, including American citizens kidnapped by ISIS and the Assad regime.
Colleagues, nearly every Syrian family has been personally affected by the widespread and systemic use of detention, torture, and killing under the Assad regime. Families of those missing, of course, deserve answers.
The United States stands with the Syrian people at this historic moment and strongly supports the Syrian government’s efforts to establish peace and prosperity. We thank the national commissions and the IIMP for updating the members of the Security Council on their work and explaining how the international community can best help them.
We also expect these national commissions—and I’ll state again—to be working in partnership with the IIMP to address abuses by all parties and operate independently, transparently, and include victims, survivors, and families.
We continue to encourage close cooperation between the Syrian government and the IIMP to help families get long-deserved answers, including for citizens of the United States and partner countries.
None of us are looking to be placated. We are looking for missing people. We are looking for results.
Enduring stability in Syria will depend on clarifying the fate and whereabouts of the more than 125,000 missing persons and advancing meaningful justice and accountability for abuses by all parties over the past 14 years.
Statements, meetings, and resolutions are important, but what happens on the ground is what matters. Syria has a remarkable opportunity to work with the IIMP bringing talent, resources, and experience that can make the difference.
As Secretary Rubio has said, a peaceful, stable Syria would be one of the most dramatic improvements in the region in a very long time, and we want to do everything we can to help achieve that.
Thank you.
