UNITED STATES MISSION TO THE UNITED NATIONS
Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE
Ambassador Mike Waltz and Tim Tebow Unite to Confront Global Child Exploitation
NEW YORK —During a high-profile briefing, the U.S. Mission to the United Nations and the Tim Tebow Foundation today shared the stark realities of international child exploitation and detailed how the United States is leading a coordinated, multi-nation offensive to hunt predators, dismantle human trafficking networks, and bring vulnerable children out of the shadows.
The briefing follows historic congressional funding wins and the success of major international operations, including Operation Renewed Hope, which have significantly strengthened the U.S. government’s capacity to combat child exploitation on a global scale. This event highlights the relentless efforts of the State Department’s Bureau of International Narcotics and Law Enforcement Affairs (INL) to bolster coordination and strengthen the international fight against these horrific crimes, including through the recent adoption of a U.S.-led resolution on combatting sextortion.
Ambassador Mike Waltz and Tim Tebow led a panel of government leaders, law enforcement officials, and anti-trafficking experts, showcasing how American technology and law enforcement surge tactics are powering an international coalition dedicated to dismantling predator networks worldwide.
“The United States is leading the charge to combat child exploitation in partnership with civil society groups like the Tim Tebow Foundation,” said Ambassador Waltz. “Child exploitation criminals operate across borders, so a global response is critical. We need to work together to protect our children from predators.”
“Child exploitation requires a response as global as the threat itself,” said Tim Tebow, Founder and Chairman of the Tim Tebow Foundation. “Right now, children around the world are being harmed, and we cannot look away. No child should be invisible, and no predator should be able to hide. I’m grateful to Ambassador Waltz, the U.S. Mission to the United Nations, and the many partners committed to protecting children. Together, we can confront this evil on a greater scale and bring more children out of darkness and into hope.”
The collaboration reflects the United States’ sustained commitment to protecting children from exploitation, both at home and abroad, through robust international partnerships, advanced technology, and coordinated law enforcement action.
