Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you to Special Representative Jenča, for your presentation.
The United States congratulates the Colombian people and authorities for holding peaceful and democratic presidential elections. We note the record voter turnout in the June 21 second round, with 63 percent of the eligible 41 million voters casting their ballots and no polling stations requiring relocation due to security concerns. We applaud the UN Verification Mission’s contributions to election security.
The United States also congratulates President-elect Abelardo de la Espriella on his victory. We look forward to working with him and his team to advance our shared priorities, including supporting national efforts to advance peace and security for all Colombians.
While election day was peaceful, we acknowledge the concerns about political violence and threats throughout the electoral cycle, including the assassination of Senator Uribe. The United States strongly reiterates its position that there is no place for political violence or meddling with the democratically expressed will of the Colombian people. Any threats to impede the democratic process and peaceful, timely, and smooth transition of power are unacceptable.
The United States remains deeply concerned about instability, violence, and illegal drug cultivation and production in Colombia. The violence and drug trafficking perpetrated by terrorist organizations and illegal armed groups threaten the safety of Colombians, of the broader region, and of Americans.
From April 24 to April 26, shortly after the last briefing to the Council on Colombia, FARC dissident terrorists unleashed a wave of violence against civilians, using improvised explosive devices, vehicle-borne explosives, and attack drones, leaving at least 21 dead. We reiterate Special Representative Jenča’s condemnation of the attack.
Eradicating coca cultivation and cocaine production is essential to preventing illegal armed groups from terrorizing Colombia and the region. The United States condemns all those who undermine peace through narcotrafficking and terrorism and looks forward to close cooperation with the De la Espriella government on these priorities.
We continue to note the tragic and wide-ranging impacts of the expanding presence of armed groups and illicit economies, including intensified violence, forced confinement, mass displacement, and the forced recruitment of children in conflict. Colombia’s deteriorating security crisis is a worrying humanitarian situation.
Establishing the conditions for long-term security will require Colombia to dedicate resources to establishing state presence in conflict zones including police, prosecutors, and judges, as well as infrastructure and access to markets for legitimate economic opportunities.
The United States is committed to peace and justice in Colombia, and our relationship with the Colombian people and their institutions is strong and enduring. We continue to urge the Government of Colombia to prioritize comprehensive and expeditious implementation of its commitments under the Peace Accord, especially those related to security and infrastructure investment, as part of the permanent solution to Colombian narcoterrorism and insecurity.
I thank you.
